  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure crests overhead today allowing for sunny skies,\nhighs in the 60s and lighter winds that what we saw on Friday. A\nseabreeze is likely on the coast this afternoon.\n\nA short wave trough will approach from the west tonight with\nrapidly increasing clouds. Increasing forcing for ascent will\naid in a shield of rain moving in from the west Sunday morning.\nThis will then spread eastward to the rest of the forecast area\nby early afternoon. There remains uncertainty in amounts at this\ntime as convection to our southwest could rob our area of deeper\nmoisture. Either way the highest amounts should be across\nsouthern NH where the rainfall could be somewhat beneficial.\n\nHourly temperatures may be non-diurnal during the day Sunday as\nthe rain shield impinges on a relatively dry air mass, i.e, \ntemperatures should fall some as the late morning and afternoon \nprogresses.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEnsemble guidance is in strong agreement that high pressure \nwill move across Canada and arrive in Quebec on Monday. This \nhigh to our north will allow for temperatures to run cooler than\nnormal through the week, with highs in the 60s and lows in the \nupper 30s and lower 40s. Frost does appear to be a possibility \nTuesday, Wednesday and Thursday nights north of the mountains.\n\nAt the end of the week, models show the potential for a coastal\nlow to impact the region. A high degree of storm track\nuncertainty remains, but the low could allow for some much \nneeded rainfall on Thursday and Friday if the low takes a more\nwesterly track.",
